  "slug": "regulation-reveals-fddd",
  "summary": "Single-cell, nascent-proteome, and yeast studies now link tRNA abundance and ribosome state to selective translational control.",
  "synthesis": "The common thread is regulation at the level of tRNA supply and ribosome state rather than transcript abundance alone. sctRNA-seq jointly profiles mRNA and tRNA abundance in single cells, linking tRNA-dependent translational control to cell identity. In beta-cells, high glucose reduces RPLP1 on translating ribosomes, impairing insulin translation and secretion, and RPLP1 knockdown causes glucose intolerance. MYC promotes pre-tRNA maturation to expand the mature tRNA pool during re-entry into proliferation in HepaRG cells. In budding yeast, nutrient repletion engages sequence-dependent ribosome pausing and tRNA charging changes during translational recovery. A labelled protein ratio normalization for nascent proteome SILAC data preserves global translation changes that median normalization masks, revealing distinct targets of eIF4A1/2 and of their inhibitor silvestrol. Together these studies position translation as a dynamically reprogrammed control layer, with methodological advances enabling its resolution.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-19"
}
